this search {{refineResultsText}} Clear Jobs Map {{jobsHeading}} {{jobsHeading}} {{jobsHeading}} and matched for resume {{selectedResume}} List Layout Card Layout Table Layout {{filtersAppliedText}} : {{op.OptionName}} Remove selection - : {{op.OptionName}} Search Criterion : {{op}} None Location Criterion : {{op}} None {{dynamicStrings.Lbl_RefineSearch.replace('[jobcount]',JobListCount)}} {{tgSettings.SortTextLabel}} {{option.LocalizedString}} Shortcuts After your selection, please press ESC key in order to apply or take other actions. Loading Featured Job Allows Referral {{dynamicStrings.Lbl_JobShare.replace('[JobTitle]',oQ.Value)}} {{dynamicStrings.Lbl_JobURL.replace('[JobTitle]',oQ.Value)}} {{dynamicStrings.Lbl_CopyJobShare.replace('[JobTitle]',oQ.Value)}} {{dynamicStrings.Lbl_CloseJobShare.replace('[JobTitle]',oQ.Value)}} There are no jobs that match your criteria {{tgSettings.ShowMoreText}} {{tgSettings.SearchResultsApplyToJobButtonText}} Save {{sendToFriendButtonText}} Refer {{filtersAppliedText}} : {{op.OptionName}} Remove selection - : {{op.OptionName}} Back You have already applied for this job. Check application status Check your applications. Application limit reached. {{LimitExceededMessage}} Close Featured Job Allows Referral Previous Job There are no previous jobs in this list. Share Recommended Jobs for You Job share URL Copy Job URL Close Job share dialog. View Similar Jobs Featured Job Allows Referral Featured Job Allows Referral Assistant Professor in Ecological and Human Health Risk Assessment 46148BR Environmental Toxicology Position Description The Department of Environmental Toxicology in the College of Arts & Sciences at Texas Tech University invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor in Ecological and Human Health Risk Assessment to begin Fall 2027. We seek a candidate whose research program advances the assessment of chemical and environmental risk to ecological and human health, with emphasis on quantitative approaches to hazard characterization, exposure assessment, and risk characterization. Candidates whose work addresses human health risk assessment or ecological risk assessment through their research are encouraged to apply. Areas of specialization include, but are not limited to: Ecological risk assessment, including species sensitivity distributions, population- and community-level effects modeling, and assessment of contaminant fate and effects in wildlife and aquatic systems. Human health risk assessment, including dose-response and benchmark dose modeling, quantitative uncertainty analysis, and integration of epidemiological or health surveillance data. Quantitative analysis of existing or newly generated datasets in support of risk assessment, including new and emerging computational approaches applied to large ecotoxicological or public health data, and cumulative or chemical mixture risk. Laboratory or field research that generates data used to inform preliminary risk estimates; programs that combine data generation with quantitative analysis are highly appreciated. Work that translates to regulatory and public health decision-making, including the assessment of chemical mixtures, is especially welcome. Research that aligns with Texas Tech University’s strategic research area of Advancing One Health is of particular interest. The successful candidate will be provided a competitive start-up package and will be expected to establish an externally funded research program supported by agencies such as EPA, NIEHS/NIH, NSF, USDA, FDA, or DoD. The successful candidate will contribute to graduate and undergraduate education by teaching courses that align with their expertise and departmental needs, including a course in ecological risk assessment, and by mentoring M.S. and Ph.D. students. A central aim of the position is to strengthen departmental training in ecological and human health risk assessment so that graduates are prepared for the quantitative demands of contemporary regulatory, industry, and academic practice. The successful candidate will benefit from collaborative opportunities within the Department of Environmental Toxicology, which maintains strengths in human and ecotoxicology, analytical and environmental chemistry, and forensic science. Depending on their expertise, the candidate could also interface with other units on campus, including the Departments of Chemistry and Biochemistry, Biological Sciences, Natural Resources Management, and Civil and Environmental Engineering, as well as the School of Veterinary Medicine, and the Texas Tech University Health Sciences Center. All prospective employees are encouraged to visit Work at Texas Tech to learn more about becoming a part of our campus community. About the University Founded in 1923, Texas Tech University began with a mission to serve the needs of West Texas, but its impact has always reached far beyond. Today, Texas Tech, located in Lubbock (pop. 300,000+), is home to a vibrant community of more than 42,000 students.Texas Tech's 1,800-acre campus showcases Spanish Renaissance architecture and is home to one of the country's largest public art collections. Its 13 colleges include a prestigious School of Law and a distinguished School of Veterinary Medicine. These programs equip students with the skills and knowledge needed to excel in their respective fields. Built on the values of West Texas — hard work, grit and authenticity — the university graduates students who are deeply engaged in service to their communities and well-positioned to succeed in the world. Texas Tech is committed to achieving research and scholarly accomplishments that compare favorably to the member institutions of the Association of American Universities (AAU). For more than 100 years, Texas Tech has been a premier destination for those seeking a world-class education and a unique, personalized experience as a member of the Red Raider family. About the College The College of Arts and Sciences is composed of 15 departments, offering a wide variety of courses and programs in the humanities, social and behavioral sciences, mathematics, physical sciences, and natural sciences. The College has more than 10,000 students enrolled representing more than a quarter of the overall Texas Tech University student population while maintaining a 22:1 student to faculty ratio. About the Department/School/Area The Department of Environmental Toxicology at Texas Tech (www.depts.ttu.edu/entx/) is a community of faculty, students, and staff committed to teaching and training students, producing innovative research that advances basic and applied science and engaging with the public. The department offers a doctoral degree in Environmental Toxicology, Master of Science degree in Environmental Toxicology and Forensic Science, and an undergraduate degree in forensic science. The department currently has 17 full-time tenured, tenure-track and professor of practice faculty and approximately 55 graduate students. In recent years, faculty from this department have been awarded over 5 million dollars in externally sponsored research, of which more than 70% were from federal sources. The department occupies several buildings to support research and teaching, affording significant laboratory and classroom space. Departmental research programs include campus, community, and national/international collaborations. Major/Essential Functions In line with TTU’s strategic priorities to engage and empower a dynamic student body, enable innovative research and creative activities, and transform lives and communities through outreach and engaged scholarship, applicants should have experience working with a breadth of student populations at the undergraduate and/or graduate levels within individual or across the areas of teaching, research/creative activity, and service. As a faculty member in the Department of Environmental Toxicology, you will be expected to: Contribute to the teaching mission of the department by developing and teaching graduate and undergraduate coursework in ecological and human health risk assessment and related areas, consistent with departmental curricular needs. Mentor and train M.S. and Ph.D. students, and serve on graduate advisory and dissertation committees. Develop a record of peer-reviewed publications and establish a sustained, externally funded research program that incorporates new and emerging computational approaches to ecological and/or human health risk assessment. Provide service to the department, college, and university, as well as to the profession. Organization Texas Tech University Faculty Qualifications Ph.D. in biology, environmental science, public health, environmental toxicology, toxicology, or a closely related discipline, with the degree conferred by September 1, 2027. A record of publications in peer-reviewed national or international scholarly journals. Research that applies or informs risk assessment for ecological receptors, human populations, or both. Use of computational or quantitative approaches within that research (e.g., predictive modeling, exposure or dose-response modeling, QSAR, PBPK/PBTK, or analysis of large toxicological datasets). Preferred Qualifications In addition to the required qualifications, individuals with the following preferred qualifications are strongly encouraged to apply: Experience applying emerging analytical methods (e.g., Big Data, machine learning, artificial intelligence, or advanced geospatial modeling) to large-scale ecotoxicological or public health datasets. Methodological skill with standard risk assessment tools, software, and databases (e.g., EPA ProUCL, R, Python, ArcGIS, PBPK modeling platforms, or the EPA CompTox Chemicals Dashboard, ECOTOX, ATSDR, and ECHA/REACH databases). A record of submitted or funded external grant applications Experience working on interdisciplinary or community-engaged research teams, especially those aligning with One Health initiatives. Evidence of preparation to teach a course in ecological and/or human health risk assessment (e.g., prior instruction, guest lectures, or a described plan for such a course). University-level teaching experience, including experience acquired as a graduate student or postdoctoral scholar. Postdoctoral, government, or industry experience related to ecological and/or human health risk assessment. Duty Point TTU Lubbock, main campus and affiliated facilities Safety Information Adherence to robust safety practices and compliance with all applicable health and safety regulations are responsibilities of all TTU employees. Does this position work in a research laboratory? Yes Special Instructions to Applicants Please include the following documents in your application: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of research interests, including a plan to develop a competitive, externally funded research program and how computational risk assessment approaches are, or would be, incorporated into that work Statement of teaching philosophy, including courses the applicant would be willing to teach One (1) representative peer-reviewed publication Contact information for three (3) professional references (letters will be requested later) Questions about this position should be directed to Dr. Jordan Crago , Search Committee Chair at jordan.crago@ttu.edu. If you need assistance with the application process, contact Human Resources, Talent Acquisition at hrs.recruiting@ttu.edu or 806-742-3851 . Job Type Full Time Pay Basis Monthly Pay Statement Compensation is commensurate upon the qualifications of the individual selected and budgetary guidelines of the hiring department, as well as the institutional pay plan. Travel Required None Grant Funded? No Job Group Assistant Professor EEO Statement All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex,  national origin, age, disability, genetic information or status as a protected veteran. Salary Grade 800 Applications must be received by 01/08/2027 Assistant Professor in Ecological and Human Health Risk Assessment | Texas Tech University View application status View your applications {{tgSettings.JobDetailsApplyButtonText}} Easy Apply Save {{jobDetailsButtonText}} Refer Show related jobs The job posting you are looking for has expired or the position has already been filled. If you are interested in one of our other opportunities, please visit our career site. We are experiencing an issue with our job search. Please try again later. Featured Job Allows Referral Next Job There are no next jobs in this list. Quick Apply Quick Apply Close Back We're sorry ... We only allow one application for this job and you have already applied to: {{ErrorMessageJobTitle}} Application limit reached. {{LimitExceededMessage}} {{ErrorMessageJobTitle}} Scroll to top"
